00:16:42,480 --> 00:16:44,480
Superintendent report, please.

246
00:16:44,480 --> 00:16:45,480
Thank you, Sheila.

247
00:16:45,480 --> 00:16:48,480
We brought to you House Bill 1300 last week.

248
00:16:48,480 --> 00:16:52,480
Just as an update, my last update to you, it was at Merrimack Superior Court.

249
00:16:52,480 --> 00:16:53,480
We kept up with the news.

250
00:16:53,480 --> 00:16:56,480
You know that that injunction was denied.

251
00:16:56,480 --> 00:17:00,480
And that question will show up in the November general election.

252
00:17:00,480 --> 00:17:07,480
If you are interested in reading that statute, it is RSA 32 colon 5 dash I.

253
00:17:07,480 --> 00:17:12,480
And what it presumes is that two questions will be on the ballot.

254
00:17:12,480 --> 00:17:18,480
It's going to have a tax cap over local property tax levy adjusted for inflation and new construction.

255
00:17:18,480 --> 00:17:26,480
And it also looks to seek to place a 6% spending cap on central office expenditures in relation to the total budget appropriations.

256
00:17:26,480 --> 00:17:32,480
In that regard, it will be a three-fifths kind of vote to approve that.

257
00:17:32,480 --> 00:17:38,480
And also a three-fifths vote down the road if it passes, if they wanted to override that.

258
00:17:38,480 --> 00:17:41,480
So that 60% marker still stands there.

259
00:17:41,480 --> 00:17:47,480
In terms of how it works here in Timberlane, the towns will run on an aggregate like it typically would.

260
00:17:47,480 --> 00:17:51,480
So it's not 60% of the voters in Plasta or 60% in Danville.

261
00:17:51,480 --> 00:17:53,480
The voters will come to the polls.

262
00:17:53,480 --> 00:17:56,480
They will be aggregated together and we will have one district percentage.

263
00:17:56,480 --> 00:17:57,480
So look out for that.

264
00:17:57,480 --> 00:17:59,480
That will be on the ballots there.

265
00:17:59,480 --> 00:18:01,480
And I'm sure the school board will be talking about that.

266
00:18:01,480 --> 00:18:06,480
I know some members are working to communicate impact if that should go forward.

267
00:18:06,480 --> 00:18:10,480
Secondly, Sheila, we talked about health care and those rates last week.

268
00:18:10,480 --> 00:18:12,480
We still expect those on October 7th.

269
00:18:12,480 --> 00:18:17,480
They released some preliminary numbers which average all health insurance plans across the state today.

270
00:18:17,480 --> 00:18:19,480
That came in at 9%.

271
00:18:19,480 --> 00:18:24,480
I don't want to say that that's a marker for what we're going to see, but that's the first public marker.

272
00:18:24,480 --> 00:18:29,480
But again, hopefully we've got some more real data to bring back to you the next meeting.

273
00:18:29,480 --> 00:18:31,480
Three things to follow up from last meeting.

274
00:18:31,480 --> 00:18:33,480
We talked about vacant positions.

275
00:18:33,480 --> 00:18:39,480
We showed you positions that were slated for contracted services and also that were busy trying to hire.

276
00:18:39,480 --> 00:18:41,480
You've got a list, I believe, that's in your packet.

277
00:18:41,480 --> 00:18:48,480
Maria's done a good job for you breaking down your request of looking at salary and benefit figures associated with those.

278
00:18:48,480 --> 00:18:50,480
Second follow up from last meeting.

279
00:18:50,480 --> 00:18:52,480
We're here to also talk with the school board tonight.

280
00:18:52,480 --> 00:18:57,480
So if you have school board goals, what they've approved for this calendar year in front of you,

281
00:18:57,480 --> 00:19:00,480
that at least you have the foundation about where the board is operating from.

282
00:19:00,480 --> 00:19:05,480
And lastly, for follow up from last meeting, we had some requests to do a different schedule.

283
00:19:05,480 --> 00:19:07,480
You'll see that, of course, reflected in the schedule tonight.

284
00:19:07,480 --> 00:19:12,480
We're bringing you four distinct and separate operating pieces of the budget before you this evening.

285
00:19:12,480 --> 00:19:15,480
That's all I have for the season, Sheila.

286
00:19:15,480 --> 00:19:17,480
Okay.

287
00:19:17,480 --> 00:19:19,480
Reports of committees.

288
00:19:19,480 --> 00:19:20,480
Anybody?

289
00:19:20,480 --> 00:19:21,480
In budget.

290
00:19:21,480 --> 00:19:22,480
No.

291
00:19:22,480 --> 00:19:27,480
How about school board?

292
00:19:27,480 --> 00:19:28,480
School board.

293
00:19:28,480 --> 00:19:31,480
Do you want school board rep?

294
00:19:31,480 --> 00:19:32,480
It says report.

295
00:19:32,480 --> 00:19:33,480
I have report of committees.

296
00:19:33,480 --> 00:19:34,480
Okay.

297
00:19:34,480 --> 00:19:39,480
Well, I'll do what I normally do for this school board rep.

298
00:19:39,480 --> 00:19:46,480
At the last school board meeting, we did make a motion and vote to move the deliberative session

299
00:19:46,480 --> 00:19:52,480
for this coming year to Saturday, February 6th, starting at 9 a.m. at the Timber Lane High School.

300
00:19:52,480 --> 00:20:01,480
The reason for the high school as opposed to the PAC is a little bit greater capacity and also ease of flow through the,

301
00:20:01,480 --> 00:20:08,480
if there is a secret ballot process, it's easier to flow through the high school areas than the PAC with the bleachers,

302
00:20:08,480 --> 00:20:13,480
not with the bleachers, with the seating going up.

303
00:20:13,480 --> 00:20:21,480
So, and we reviewed financially, the only other action item, we reviewed the budget to actual report,

304
00:20:21,480 --> 00:20:28,480
which it's early days, so that report shows spending today in projections for the year.

305
00:20:28,480 --> 00:20:34,480
It will become more meaningful as the year goes on, but it's very helpful to have a consistent format.

306
00:20:34,480 --> 00:20:36,480
Every month we see that report.

307
00:20:36,480 --> 00:20:43,480
So, those were the action items that I thought were relevant to the budget committee.

308
00:20:43,480 --> 00:20:46,480
Is there anybody else on the school board that has anything?

309
00:20:46,480 --> 00:20:48,480
I think you're running a good meeting so far.

310
00:20:48,480 --> 00:20:49,480
Okay.

311
00:20:49,480 --> 00:20:50,480
Okay.

312
00:20:50,480 --> 00:20:55,480
Now we have new business.

313
00:20:55,480 --> 00:20:57,480
Open forum discussion.

314
00:20:57,480 --> 00:21:03,480
Budget committee priorities, school board committee priorities.

315
00:21:03,480 --> 00:21:10,480
I am going to let the school board go first on this, seeing they have it all typed up pretty.

316
00:21:10,480 --> 00:21:12,480
They'd like to talk on it.

317
00:21:12,480 --> 00:21:13,480
Sure.

318
00:21:13,480 --> 00:21:14,480
Hey, Sheila.

319
00:21:14,480 --> 00:21:15,480
It's okay if I go?

320
00:21:15,480 --> 00:21:16,480
Go ahead.

321
00:21:16,480 --> 00:21:17,480
Tell you.

322
00:21:17,480 --> 00:21:18,480
No, I know.

323
00:21:18,480 --> 00:21:19,480
It's way down there.

324
00:21:19,480 --> 00:21:32,480
So, we went from, we tried to consolidate a great deal from 20 some odd different goals to three discrete colors.

325
00:21:32,480 --> 00:21:33,480
I think this is our focus.

326
00:21:33,480 --> 00:21:38,480
Doing what we can to improve student academic achievement life itself.

327
00:21:38,480 --> 00:21:45,480
For, with an increased emphasis not just on preparation for college, but also for our career and civic life.

328
00:21:45,480 --> 00:21:51,480
For the past couple years, if you've been watching, there's been a real emphasis on some of the non-traditional learning routes.

329
00:21:51,480 --> 00:21:59,480
The lowers that we've had come in and providing access to students who may not be traditionally academically inclined.

330
00:21:59,480 --> 00:22:11,480
It may not be taking the boat, the traditional trades route, but really having a passion for increasing the knowledge and their ability to enter the workforce in a non-traditional way.

331
00:22:11,480 --> 00:22:12,480
That falls under the learning.

332
00:22:12,480 --> 00:22:27,480
Under community connection and communication, there has been an acknowledgment over the past couple years that prior to this, the school board in general, preceding my time and including my time in the board, has done a suboptimal job in community.

333
00:22:27,480 --> 00:22:34,480
Our goals and what is happening within the district and the decisions we are making.

334
00:22:34,480 --> 00:22:49,480
So this year, again, we have charged the superintendent to increase his communication and have the SAU increase their communication out to the public so that they, whether they have students in the district or not, have more access to additional information.

335
00:22:49,480 --> 00:22:58,480
Part of that has been headed up by Ken through technology to make information easier to find instead of going through Pan's Labyrinth to find the easiest reports.

336
00:22:58,480 --> 00:23:00,480
It's been streamlined considerably.

337
00:23:00,480 --> 00:23:04,480
The superintendent has also started something which I think is phenomenal.

338
00:23:04,480 --> 00:23:07,480
The superintendent has done.

339
00:23:07,480 --> 00:23:13,480
Appearing in front of our BOSs on a regular basis to give them kind of a State of the Union and an update.

340
00:23:13,480 --> 00:23:23,480
This is a chance for him to go out to the greater community to speak to our elected officials and to the greater public in our towns who are not necessarily directly affiliated with Timberlane.

341
00:23:23,480 --> 00:23:32,480
So I think some of the mailings that was referred to during the delegates and individuals was part of the informational charge that we gave him.

342
00:23:32,480 --> 00:23:33,480
Get the information out.

343
00:23:33,480 --> 00:23:46,480
We want more public involvement and more public knowledge, be it far deliberative mailings through the district or actually going to the board of selectmen and asking them to post it on every town website just so that there is greater information.

344
00:23:46,480 --> 00:24:01,480
So that's part of the community connection and obviously facilities and the learning environment as everybody here knows our district was forced into taking out a lease that I think we all wish we did not have to swallow that bitter pill.

345
00:24:01,480 --> 00:24:10,480
Unfortunately, after, you know, a decade of neglect of our physical structures, the building was falling, the buildings were falling apart.

346
00:24:10,480 --> 00:24:19,480
They needed some, not just a last job, but they needed Bondo to replace some of the siding that had been rotting off for years.

347
00:24:19,480 --> 00:24:22,480
The lease has done a phenomenal job.

348
00:24:22,480 --> 00:24:30,480
We've used that pretty well to take care of the most challenging structural and facility needs that we have.

349
00:24:30,480 --> 00:24:33,480
Uh, I, I think we're getting toward the end of that road.

350
00:24:33,480 --> 00:24:40,480
Uh, the last being the envelope of the building and, you know, we have some big, uh, questions regarding window replacement, et cetera.

351
00:24:40,480 --> 00:24:56,480
Uh, we realize that, uh, as a board, we realize the financial burden that doing all this work at once, which should have been spread out year after year over decades, having it all put in, you know, over one or two year period, uh, is, is quite a mouthful to chew.

352
00:24:56,480 --> 00:24:58,480
Uh, and a lot of people are gagging on that mouthful.

353
00:24:58,480 --> 00:25:00,480
It would have been easy to take little lips.

354
00:25:00,480 --> 00:25:10,480
Uh, I'm hoping in the future, the school board in conjunction with facilities and CIP are able to lay out a more structured plan so that everything doesn't, doesn't happen at once.

355
00:25:10,480 --> 00:25:12,480
Uh, those are the three main tiers.

356
00:25:12,480 --> 00:25:17,480
Anybody else on the school board?

357
00:25:17,480 --> 00:25:25,480
I would like to follow up from what Mark was saying about our goal, the, um, strengthening engagement with the member communities and stakeholders.

358
00:25:25,480 --> 00:25:38,480
But I'd like to focus on the rest of that sentence, which is maintain a sustainable financial approach that balances educational excellence, taxpayer affordability, and the long-term viability of the district.

359
00:25:38,480 --> 00:25:55,480
And I think those three pillars are the things that, um, we need to focus on, not just, um, we obviously were in the business to provide educational excellence, um, but taxpayer affordability, that's where most of our money to do so comes from.

360
00:25:55,480 --> 00:26:07,480
We have to keep that in mind and long-term viability, there are trends in this, um, state and in this nation that are, um, financial burdens, um, to provide public education.

361
00:26:07,480 --> 00:26:19,480
And those are not unique to Timberlane, but at Timberlane, we need to, um, I think do what we can to, um, manage the impact of things that are going on.

362
00:26:19,480 --> 00:26:27,480
And, um, and, um, I just want to give a shout out to Shauna Manthorne because she wrote this sentence and I think it really encapsulates what we're all about.

363
00:26:27,480 --> 00:26:35,480
So, um, that, um, so what I'd like to encourage the budget committee is to keep an open mind and ask questions along the way.

364
00:26:35,480 --> 00:26:48,480
Um, I've watched two budget processes now and, um, it seemed like it wasn't until the end that any, anything really started to happen where people said, what about this?

365
00:26:48,480 --> 00:26:49,480
What about that?

366
00:26:49,480 --> 00:26:54,480
Um, you get presentations, um, every two weeks, ask the questions.

367
00:26:54,480 --> 00:26:55,480
Okay.

368
00:26:55,480 --> 00:27:04,480
I know the, um, SAU, um, representatives, the departments who are here are willing to answer the questions and will, if they can't answer them that night, they'll come back to you.

369
00:27:04,480 --> 00:27:09,480
Um, so, um, that would be my, my hope for this year.

370
00:27:09,480 --> 00:27:17,480
Um, uh, I think the change of order of presentations is going to be very beneficial because the bigger parts of the budget are coming up earlier in the year.

371
00:27:17,480 --> 00:27:21,480
So there's time for those questions to be researched and answered.

372
00:27:21,480 --> 00:27:27,480
And, um, and lastly, I would say, um, take advantage of the specialized workshop.

373
00:27:27,480 --> 00:27:28,480
You talked about one last meeting.

374
00:27:28,480 --> 00:27:39,480
Um, and if you aren't familiar with the whole apportionment, how to, how do we get from a budget to the taxes and, um, all those details and what goes into it.

375
00:27:39,480 --> 00:27:46,480
Um, and, um, how the articles of agreement combined with New Hampshire law, you don't know that process yet.

376
00:27:46,480 --> 00:27:54,480
Um, um, and I didn't, when I came to the school board, please, um, reach out and, um, and get that background.

377
00:27:54,480 --> 00:28:12,480
And if you have individual questions, I know, um, everybody at the SIU office and also, you know, particularly Maria and Justin have been very, very willing to answer any of my questions as I became, um, familiar with everything going on in the district.

378
00:28:12,480 --> 00:28:13,480
Thank you.

379
00:28:13,480 --> 00:28:14,480
Thank you.

380
00:28:14,480 --> 00:28:27,480
Um, I just want to piggyback on what Mark said about chipping away at facilities a little bit at a time for God, since for the last 15 years, I've always suggested, and I'll make this recommendation again.

381
00:28:27,480 --> 00:28:32,480
I don't think we can do it right now that when it comes to facilities, there's a formula out there.

382
00:28:32,480 --> 00:28:41,480
It's, it's well known to use across the country that you base your, your expenditures on the total replacement value of your assets.

383
00:28:41,480 --> 00:28:48,480
So you have a building, the total replacement value could take 3% of that and it's whatever that percentage is.

384
00:28:48,480 --> 00:28:50,480
And that's for the day to day maintenance of the buildings.

385
00:28:50,480 --> 00:28:56,480
You take a smaller percentage for at the end of the year, you're going to fix doors and windows, that kind of stuff.

386
00:28:56,480 --> 00:29:02,480
And then you tuck away 1% for lack of a better term for, for the, you know, for a roof replacement, that kind of stuff.

387
00:29:02,480 --> 00:29:04,480
It's it's the model is out there.

388
00:29:04,480 --> 00:29:06,480
You can find it and research it.

389
00:29:06,480 --> 00:29:11,480
What I think that accomplishes, it prevents these peaks and valleys, right?

390
00:29:11,480 --> 00:29:17,480
You, you, things don't come up and they're not a surprise and you kind of already know what you're going to be spending.

391
00:29:17,480 --> 00:29:19,480
Cause you know what that total replacement value is.

392
00:29:19,480 --> 00:29:25,480
Um, and as far as not letting things go for decades, I think it's important to mention.

393
00:29:25,480 --> 00:29:29,480
That we're working on facility audits, things of that nature.

394
00:29:29,480 --> 00:29:30,480
That.

395
00:29:30,480 --> 00:29:31,480
Will.

396
00:29:31,480 --> 00:29:32,480
It will just come up.

397
00:29:32,480 --> 00:29:34,480
This needs to be replaced.

398
00:29:34,480 --> 00:29:35,480
This needs to be fixed.

399
00:29:35,480 --> 00:29:42,480
Uh, you know, and then I guess there's some discretion involved in that, but there are processes being put in place to avoid.

400
00:29:42,480 --> 00:29:47,480
What we just had to go through for the last seven, eight years or so.

401
00:29:47,480 --> 00:30:01,480
So I, I think not only have we addressed a lot of things that have been broken and I think we're almost out of the woods, but we're also addressing future needs and how to manage those in a more productive and more efficient way.

402
00:30:01,480 --> 00:30:10,480
And more fiscally sound way, um, because if you don't invest now, it's going to cost you more later.

403
00:30:10,480 --> 00:30:13,480
Um, so that's it.

404
00:30:13,480 --> 00:30:14,480
Okay.

405
00:30:14,480 --> 00:30:16,480
Anybody else on the school board?

406
00:30:16,480 --> 00:30:18,480
Anything going on want to say?

407
00:30:18,480 --> 00:30:19,480
Woody.

408
00:30:19,480 --> 00:30:20,480
Pretty much one.

409
00:30:20,480 --> 00:30:48,480
The last thing you said, Jack, uh, was kind of the first thing I would have said is that, you know, oftentimes we end up paying profound, increasingly profound amounts of money to, uh, to pay for something that if we had done it when it was recognized as a need initially, it would have been, I would say, maybe a third in some cases of, of, of, of the cost.

410
00:30:48,480 --> 00:30:49,480
Sometimes even less.

411
00:30:49,480 --> 00:30:58,480
It's just, we, people come up with needs, um, and certainly not everything is going to always be afforded.

412
00:30:58,480 --> 00:31:05,480
But what we faced in the past was that there'd be like an arbitrary amount set.

413
00:31:05,480 --> 00:31:10,480
A bunch of committee felt like, oh, we could do 600,000 this year.

414
00:31:10,480 --> 00:31:21,480
But we had 3 million, 4 million, you know, uh, of agreed upon things that we had prioritized through capital improvement plan and facilities, communities and whatnot.

415
00:31:21,480 --> 00:31:26,480
And so there'd be impossible choices.

416
00:31:26,480 --> 00:31:31,480
In fact, nothing was at the price point that, you know, that $600,000 really meant anything.

417
00:31:31,480 --> 00:31:42,480
So you, you pick some things and sometimes you'd come in under that and you'd be actually handing money back or looking for something that you could afford to do that you needed to do, you know, with that remaining money.

418
00:31:42,480 --> 00:31:47,480
I hate to see us continue that type of, you know, approach.

419
00:31:47,480 --> 00:31:58,480
I think we have to really take a look at making plans, you know, to achieve and sticking with the plans to achieve the, the needed work, uh, and try to address it in a timely way.

420
00:31:58,480 --> 00:32:13,480
So it doesn't compound and become more work and with more deterioration and the, the facility at the, the schools, the outside, the envelope of these buildings is a perfect example of that.

421
00:32:13,480 --> 00:32:15,480
These things are not new things.

422
00:32:15,480 --> 00:32:16,480
Windows is not a new thing.

423
00:32:16,480 --> 00:32:19,480
That's been something that's been 20 years.

424
00:32:19,480 --> 00:32:20,480
We've been talking about replacing those.

425
00:32:20,480 --> 00:32:23,480
Sheila, you, you've been a part of that in some of those committees.

426
00:32:23,480 --> 00:32:24,480
You, you've seen this happen.

427
00:32:24,480 --> 00:32:25,480
So, you know, I'm not kidding.

428
00:32:25,480 --> 00:32:27,480
No, I agree with you wholeheartedly.

429
00:32:27,480 --> 00:32:30,480
So it's, it's not a one sentence thing.

430
00:32:30,480 --> 00:32:41,480
It's, it's an important concept to grasp that we're trying to save the money long-term and people are looking short-term and that I think is a mistake.

431
00:32:41,480 --> 00:32:55,480
Uh, you know, we, we try to do the best we can with what we end up agreeing to, and it's always going to be less than we want, but it shouldn't be so much that it cripples us, uh, from, from being able to do the work.

432
00:32:55,480 --> 00:32:58,480
Okay.

433
00:32:58,480 --> 00:32:59,480
Anybody else?

434
00:32:59,480 --> 00:33:00,480
School board?

435
00:33:00,480 --> 00:33:01,480
Anybody?

436
00:33:01,480 --> 00:33:02,480
Okay.

437
00:33:02,480 --> 00:33:03,480
Budget committee.

438
00:33:03,480 --> 00:33:04,480
Anybody want to speak?

439
00:33:04,480 --> 00:33:05,480
I will.

440
00:33:05,480 --> 00:33:16,480
I'll say we should probably keep that those comments in mind when we're going through our budget, because I feel often the CIP is the first thing that's slashed as a way to reduce the budget.

441
00:33:16,480 --> 00:33:26,480
And it doesn't seem to be helping with the long-term viability of the district and, um, maintaining the value of our facilities and assets.

442
00:33:26,480 --> 00:33:29,480
I'm going to go next.

443
00:33:29,480 --> 00:33:35,480
To me, it depends on what's coming through from that CIP committee.

444
00:33:35,480 --> 00:33:40,480
That's what I make my choices on because I'll be completely honest with you.

445
00:33:40,480 --> 00:33:45,480
And I've said it in CIP when I had to be Ryan's backup one day.

446
00:33:45,480 --> 00:33:49,480
Yeah, you forgot last week.

447
00:33:49,480 --> 00:33:55,480
Um, it depends on what's coming through for me on those CIP committees.

448
00:33:55,480 --> 00:34:01,480
To be perfectly honest, you sent me through windows, probably keep that right in there.

449
00:34:01,480 --> 00:34:05,480
You sent me through field houses.

450
00:34:05,480 --> 00:34:07,480
It's going.

451
00:34:07,480 --> 00:34:08,480
Just saying.

452
00:34:08,480 --> 00:34:10,480
That's my way of looking at it.

453
00:34:10,480 --> 00:34:15,480
Because that to me is champagne taste on a beer budget.

454
00:34:15,480 --> 00:34:16,480
We need other things.

455
00:34:16,480 --> 00:34:17,480
Can I ask clarification?

456
00:34:17,480 --> 00:34:19,480
Because I don't know what the, um, what are they?

457
00:34:19,480 --> 00:34:20,480
Field houses.

458
00:34:20,480 --> 00:34:23,480
Do they store the materials like that we bought for the sports?

459
00:34:23,480 --> 00:34:24,480
I don't know.

460
00:34:24,480 --> 00:34:25,480
Field houses.

461
00:34:25,480 --> 00:34:26,480
I don't know what it is.

462
00:34:26,480 --> 00:34:27,480
If it's a restaurant.

463
00:34:27,480 --> 00:34:29,480
Then you track basketball courts.

464
00:34:29,480 --> 00:34:30,480
Oh, okay.

465
00:34:30,480 --> 00:34:31,480
That we use daily.

466
00:34:31,480 --> 00:34:32,480
Yeah.

467
00:34:32,480 --> 00:34:34,480
I think it's like $600,000.

468
00:34:34,480 --> 00:34:36,480
It's right now on the CIP.

469
00:34:36,480 --> 00:34:37,480
To maintain that.

470
00:34:37,480 --> 00:34:38,480
That's a storage.

471
00:34:38,480 --> 00:34:39,480
That's a storage.

472
00:34:39,480 --> 00:34:40,480
It's just storage.

473
00:34:40,480 --> 00:34:42,480
It's storing things that cost money.

474
00:34:42,480 --> 00:34:43,480
I want to know if we're.

475
00:34:43,480 --> 00:34:47,480
There's a lot of members on both groups that sit on that committee.

476
00:34:47,480 --> 00:34:50,480
Has that committee had discussions about what projects they anticipate bringing?

477
00:34:50,480 --> 00:34:53,480
So let me give you an update being the co-chair.

478
00:34:53,480 --> 00:34:57,480
We have right now finalized.

479
00:34:57,480 --> 00:35:01,480
I think all the items minus one item.

480
00:35:01,480 --> 00:35:02,480
And we're waiting.

481
00:35:02,480 --> 00:35:07,480
We won't have that information until the 14th, which are coincidentally windows.

482
00:35:07,480 --> 00:35:14,480
After we get that information, we will be, uh, we will be doing a follow up meeting.

483
00:35:14,480 --> 00:35:15,480
And actually talk to Dr.

484
00:35:15,480 --> 00:35:16,480
Mine about the timing of all of that.

485
00:35:16,480 --> 00:35:21,480
And so we're going to have a meeting after the 14th, after the school board meeting that

486
00:35:21,480 --> 00:35:27,480
will be able to finalize our, our list and prioritize it because in the old days, we just

487
00:35:27,480 --> 00:35:28,480
give you a list.

488
00:35:28,480 --> 00:35:31,480
Now we're saying, here's what we think is number one, number two, number three, number

489
00:35:31,480 --> 00:35:32,480
four.

490
00:35:32,480 --> 00:35:37,480
And we present, I believe it's the 28th of the 29th of the budget committee.

491
00:35:37,480 --> 00:35:38,480
That's what I was just looking at about.

492
00:35:38,480 --> 00:35:39,480
Yeah.

493
00:35:39,480 --> 00:35:41,480
I think it's the 28th off the top of my head.

494
00:35:41,480 --> 00:35:42,480
So we will have that list.

495
00:35:42,480 --> 00:35:48,480
It will be prioritized for this group for the budget committee during that meeting, either

496
00:35:48,480 --> 00:35:50,480
the 28th or 29th.

497
00:35:50,480 --> 00:35:52,480
So that's the kind of the sequence of events.

498
00:35:52,480 --> 00:35:57,480
But we really, until we get that number from the architect, we just kind of got to sit tight,

499
00:35:57,480 --> 00:36:02,480
but we have it condensed down to the right number, give or take.

500
00:36:02,480 --> 00:36:07,480
And if we have to make a subtraction due to, or who knows, maybe in addition, if we come

501
00:36:07,480 --> 00:36:13,480
in under, wouldn't that be a surprise, but a surprise, but I think it was a well thought

502
00:36:13,480 --> 00:36:14,480
out list.

503
00:36:14,480 --> 00:36:19,480
Some of those items that are on that list have been there for close to a decade.

504
00:36:19,480 --> 00:36:26,480
And we've just done without, and some of them are being coordinated with other projects

505
00:36:26,480 --> 00:36:27,480
like the envelope.

506
00:36:27,480 --> 00:36:30,480
So it's just a sequence of events.

507
00:36:30,480 --> 00:36:33,480
So yeah, we're in good shape.

508
00:36:33,480 --> 00:36:35,480
So that was my answer to that.

509
00:36:35,480 --> 00:36:37,480
Depends on what's on the CIP.

510
00:36:37,480 --> 00:36:41,480
Because I want the money spent well.

511
00:36:41,480 --> 00:36:43,480
It's as simple as that.

512
00:36:43,480 --> 00:36:46,480
Spent well on the buildings.

513
00:36:46,480 --> 00:36:49,480
I made this very clear a long time ago.

514
00:36:49,480 --> 00:36:51,480
I'm all set with outside of the buildings.

515
00:36:51,480 --> 00:36:53,480
They've gotten them, nothing is it going to get.

516
00:36:53,480 --> 00:36:57,480
I want money going into the buildings, windows, e-handlers.

517
00:36:57,480 --> 00:37:02,480
Because I understand we have some e-handlers that are dying in the high school.

518
00:37:02,480 --> 00:37:05,480
So that's what's important.

519
00:37:05,480 --> 00:37:06,480
Yeah.

520
00:37:06,480 --> 00:37:08,480
To me, that's the line.

521
00:37:08,480 --> 00:37:13,480
That's also being addressed, Sheila.

522
00:37:13,480 --> 00:37:14,480
FYI.

523
00:37:14,480 --> 00:37:15,480
I'm sorry?

524
00:37:15,480 --> 00:37:16,480
That's also being addressed.

525
00:37:16,480 --> 00:37:17,480
That's good.

526
00:37:17,480 --> 00:37:18,480
Yeah.

527
00:37:18,480 --> 00:37:19,480
That needs to be addressed.

528
00:37:19,480 --> 00:37:23,480
This is where the kids are living for six, seven hours a day.

529
00:37:23,480 --> 00:37:27,480
Anybody else on Budcom that wants to say anything?

530
00:37:27,480 --> 00:37:33,480
I just want to say that I think that the goal that the school board brought forward sounds

531
00:37:33,480 --> 00:37:38,480
similar, I would think, at least to what the budget committee is thinking as well.

532
00:37:38,480 --> 00:37:45,480
And I hope moving forward that we can remember that this is our goal and to work collaboratively

533
00:37:45,480 --> 00:37:48,480
and not have such animosity towards one another.

534
00:37:48,480 --> 00:37:55,480
And remember that we're looking to do all of these things together for all of us, for

535
00:37:55,480 --> 00:37:59,480
the taxpayers, for affordability, for our students, and for our buildings.

536
00:37:59,480 --> 00:38:05,480
And I think that the more we come across as us versus them and you versus me, we're not

537
00:38:05,480 --> 00:38:06,480
going to get anywhere.

538
00:38:06,480 --> 00:38:12,480
And we have to look at, like Mary Ann brought up, like it's across the nation.

539
00:38:12,480 --> 00:38:15,480
Public education is in dire trouble everywhere.

540
00:38:15,480 --> 00:38:20,480
And we're going to continue to have strains on school districts.

541
00:38:20,480 --> 00:38:22,480
So we have to think creatively.

542
00:38:22,480 --> 00:38:28,480
We have to work together and hear each other in our priorities because we're all going to

543
00:38:28,480 --> 00:38:37,480
have different priorities, but understand that we're all coming from a place of caring about

544
00:38:37,480 --> 00:38:41,480
students, about our homes, about being able to afford to live.

545
00:38:41,480 --> 00:38:43,480
I think we're all on the same team.

546
00:38:43,480 --> 00:38:49,480
And if we continue to move forward in this attitude of trying to throw each other under

547
00:38:49,480 --> 00:38:51,480
the bus, we're not going to get anywhere.

548
00:38:51,480 --> 00:38:53,480
And it's only going to get harder.

549
00:38:53,480 --> 00:38:55,480
That's it.

550
00:38:55,480 --> 00:38:56,480
Okay.

551
00:38:56,480 --> 00:38:57,480
Anybody else?

552
00:38:57,480 --> 00:38:58,480
Thank you.

553
00:38:58,480 --> 00:38:59,480
Thank you.

554
00:38:59,480 --> 00:39:08,480
$82 million budget was approved and just under $82 million spent for the fiscal year 25-26.

555
00:39:08,480 --> 00:39:12,480
I want to commend the SAA for coming under budget.

556
00:39:12,480 --> 00:39:18,480
FY 25-26 budget proves coming in under budget is possible.

557
00:39:18,480 --> 00:39:19,480
It can be done.

558
00:39:19,480 --> 00:39:23,480
As we begin this budget season, I believe it's important to remember that there are three

559
00:39:23,480 --> 00:39:28,480
major stakeholders in this process, students, staff, and taxpayers.

560
00:39:28,480 --> 00:39:34,480
Students and staff are often the forefront of these discussions, but taxpayers are often overlooked.

561
00:39:34,480 --> 00:39:37,480
Without the taxpayers, there is no Timberlane Regional School District.

562
00:39:37,480 --> 00:39:44,480
They deserve the same respect and consideration and are equally important as the other stakeholders.

563
00:39:44,480 --> 00:39:49,480
At a school board meeting during the past year, it was stated that the Timberlane Regional School District

564
00:39:49,480 --> 00:39:55,480
had never had to make these tough choices, referring to the $82 million budget that they were working with.

565
00:39:55,480 --> 00:39:57,480
My response is simple.

566
00:39:57,480 --> 00:40:00,480
Taxpayers make tough choices every day.

567
00:40:00,480 --> 00:40:07,480
With the rising costs of food utilities, rising property taxes, families are tightening their household budgets.

568
00:40:07,480 --> 00:40:12,480
Many residents, particularly elderly residents, are struggling with increasing property taxes.

569
00:40:12,480 --> 00:40:21,480
Many of our senior residents, as well as struggling families, are being forced out of their homes due to rising property taxes.

570
00:40:21,480 --> 00:40:28,480
We need to be mindful and much more proactive on the impact of our school budget that it has on the people who are paying for it.

571
00:40:28,480 --> 00:40:31,480
Let's keep our elderly residents and families in their homes.

572
00:40:31,480 --> 00:40:37,480
And there's no better way to support our students and families than to ensure that they have a stable home to return to.

573
00:40:37,480 --> 00:40:45,480
My goal this budget season is to participate in the process with all three stakeholders in mind, students, staff, and taxpayers.

574
00:40:45,480 --> 00:40:54,480
I believe we should strive for reasonable and responsible spending as low as possible while providing students with what they need to succeed.

575
00:40:54,480 --> 00:41:01,480
The FY25-26 budget shows that we can make responsible choices and still provide a quality education.

576
00:41:01,480 --> 00:41:09,480
My goal is to help develop and recommend a budget that balances educational needs with the financial realities facing our taxpayers.

577
00:41:09,480 --> 00:41:15,480
Higher school spending and larger school budgets directly affect property taxes.

578
00:41:15,480 --> 00:41:24,480
Our students deserve quality education and our taxpayers deserve consideration for the financial burden that they are carrying.

579
00:41:24,480 --> 00:41:30,480
Truth in budgeting is essential and transparency is essential to maintaining public trust.

580
00:41:30,480 --> 00:41:33,480
Trust is earned through honesty, truthfulness, and transparency.

581
00:41:33,480 --> 00:41:40,480
Once public trust is lost, it's difficult to regain.

582
00:41:40,480 --> 00:41:50,480
Recent right to know information made public regarding the 26 budget season and deliberative session has raised concerns about transparency involving the Timberlane Regional School District.

583
00:41:50,480 --> 00:41:53,480
Both are directly involved in the budget process.

584
00:41:53,480 --> 00:42:00,480
These communications leading up to deliberative have contributed concerns and a loss of public trust.

585
00:42:00,480 --> 00:42:03,480
This information was provided by Right to Know and is now public.

586
00:42:03,480 --> 00:42:06,480
It raises questions that should be addressed as we move forward.

587
00:42:06,480 --> 00:42:16,480
A successful budget process depends not only on the numbers but also on the public's confidence that the process is honest, transparent, and accountable.

588
00:42:16,480 --> 00:42:21,480
That will be especially important as we move forward in the 27-28 budget process.

589
00:42:21,480 --> 00:42:27,480
Taxpayers throughout the district, but particularly those in the two smaller towns, are feeling financial pressure.

590
00:42:27,480 --> 00:42:31,480
That is what I will keep in the forefront throughout this budget process.

591
00:42:31,480 --> 00:42:42,480
Responsible spending, quality education, and respect for every stakeholder, especially the taxpayers who fund our schools.

592
00:42:42,480 --> 00:42:46,480
Anybody else?

593
00:42:46,480 --> 00:42:48,480
Okay, Brian.

594
00:42:48,480 --> 00:42:51,480
So I'm thinking about priorities.

595
00:42:51,480 --> 00:42:57,480
It's no surprise to those that know me that cost reduction is in the foreground of that.

596
00:42:57,480 --> 00:43:01,480
The earlier increased budgets haven't increased results.

597
00:43:01,480 --> 00:43:09,480
We can do more with less if we get creative.

598
00:43:09,480 --> 00:43:27,480
Creativity requires the ability for those to change, and I don't think the budgets over the last several years have changed much of anything other than regurgitating the same things over and over.

599
00:43:27,480 --> 00:43:28,480
So I think that's a good point.

600
00:43:28,480 --> 00:43:29,480
I think that's a good point.

601
00:43:29,480 --> 00:43:33,480
I think that's a good point to the point of capital improvement, renovation, construction.

602
00:43:33,480 --> 00:43:34,480
I'm a construction guy.

603
00:43:34,480 --> 00:43:38,480
I wholeheartedly support maintaining buildings and infrastructure.

604
00:43:38,480 --> 00:43:45,480
I don't support expanding those in any way at this point, given the public challenges and the cost challenges.

605
00:43:45,480 --> 00:44:03,480
With that said, speaking to the it's the first thing that gets cut, well, we need to make tough decisions across the board to allow for there to be space for renovations and capital care of the facilities.

606
00:44:03,480 --> 00:44:13,480
So that could include reducing staff, could include reducing SAU staff, could include reducing teacher staff, support staff, et cetera.

607
00:44:13,480 --> 00:44:15,480
So are those difficult decisions to make?

608
00:44:15,480 --> 00:44:16,480
Absolutely.

609
00:44:16,480 --> 00:44:24,480
People's livelihoods, they're not easy decisions, but all businesses and all entities have to make those decisions at times.

610
00:44:24,480 --> 00:44:27,480
And it's not the end of the world.

611
00:44:27,480 --> 00:44:30,480
I've been let go of jobs before.

612
00:44:30,480 --> 00:44:36,480
It's quite honestly made me a better person as a result in the end.

613
00:44:36,480 --> 00:44:53,480
Short-term trials and tribulations, of course, but this needs to be sustainable for all the districts, all the towns involved, and all the constituents that support it.

614
00:44:53,480 --> 00:45:00,480
And that's not saying you just, you know, cut for cutting sake.

615
00:45:00,480 --> 00:45:09,480
It's saying that in order to prioritize one thing, you need to make concessions somewhere else.

616
00:45:09,480 --> 00:45:11,480
And that's the way the process works.

617
00:45:11,480 --> 00:45:25,480
Regarding Carol's comment, regarding the, you know, meeting the $82 million budget, every year this district and its leadership should be challenged to meet the budget.

618
00:45:25,480 --> 00:45:29,480
It should be a give and take.

619
00:45:29,480 --> 00:45:33,480
It shouldn't be, we have room for everything.

620
00:45:33,480 --> 00:45:35,480
It's not the way budgets work.

621
00:45:35,480 --> 00:45:38,480
It's not the way they work in capital projects.

622
00:45:38,480 --> 00:45:42,480
It's not the way they work in businesses either.

623
00:45:42,480 --> 00:45:49,480
The district's job is to challenge themselves to do better with less.

624
00:45:49,480 --> 00:45:53,480
And that would be a priority that I have.

625
00:45:53,480 --> 00:46:01,480
Regarding some specifics on budget review, I've taken a look at some of these packets and there's some good detail, but there's not enough detail.

626
00:46:01,480 --> 00:46:05,480
You know, when I look at a budget, I want to see everything in it.

627
00:46:05,480 --> 00:46:13,480
Whatever that department had used to create that budget spreadsheet of every item, we should have access to it.

628
00:46:13,480 --> 00:46:15,480
And we should be able to look at it.

629
00:46:15,480 --> 00:46:21,480
I'm not saying I'm going to suggest that we reduce down from 12 pencils to six pencils.

630
00:46:21,480 --> 00:46:27,480
It's getting a little ridiculous, but there should be some better detail behind the numbers we're seeing.

631
00:46:27,480 --> 00:46:31,480
And that detail should be able to be scrutinized by this group.

632
00:46:31,480 --> 00:46:35,480
Because if it's not scrutinized by this group, then who's scrutinized by it?

633
00:46:35,480 --> 00:46:37,480
That's what we're here for.

634
00:46:37,480 --> 00:46:43,480
So I think the information provided should be the greatest level detail available.

635
00:46:43,480 --> 00:46:45,480
It shouldn't be in PDF format.

636
00:46:45,480 --> 00:46:59,480
It should be in Excel format so we can actually use tools to analyze things as opposed to punching numbers into calculators and manually typing things into spreadsheets and things of that nature.

637
00:46:59,480 --> 00:47:04,480
Make it easier for this committee to be able to review the numbers provided.

638
00:47:04,480 --> 00:47:14,480
For the metrics that are provided, the charts and graphs, they're really just pretty pictures without knowing what the data is driving behind them.

639
00:47:14,480 --> 00:47:17,480
So if you provide a chart and graph, give us the data.

640
00:47:17,480 --> 00:47:22,480
Show us how you develop that chart and graph to instill confidence in the numbers that you're showing.

641
00:47:22,480 --> 00:47:29,480
I quite frankly don't have confidence in most of the charts and graphs that are presented.

642
00:47:29,480 --> 00:47:44,480
And then I think something that hasn't happened in the past is I think as a budget committee, we need to look at budgets and not be afraid of rejecting the budget outright.

643
00:47:44,480 --> 00:47:55,480
And just sending it back and telling whatever department, roll up your sleeves, sharpen your pencils, come back to us with something more appropriate.

644
00:47:55,480 --> 00:48:10,480
And what I've seen in the hours I've spent reviewing the information we have today, line items are routinely 40 to 50% above the historical averages over the last four or five years.

645
00:48:10,480 --> 00:48:12,480
That's death by a thousand cuts.

646
00:48:12,480 --> 00:48:16,480
And so when we talk about, well, there's nothing to cut.

647
00:48:16,480 --> 00:48:21,480
Well, if every line item is inflated, we end up with an inflated budget.

648
00:48:21,480 --> 00:48:32,480
So we need to challenge each and every line item and we need to provide what's reasonable, not, not what is lofty.

649
00:48:32,480 --> 00:48:45,480
I want to make sure my budget committee will speak first.

650
00:48:45,480 --> 00:48:46,480
You guys haven't spoken.

651
00:48:46,480 --> 00:48:50,480
So if either one of you want to speak, please do.

652
00:48:50,480 --> 00:48:55,480
So I'm just going to be super brief here.

653
00:48:55,480 --> 00:49:13,480
I think if I take bits and pieces of what everybody said here, what I would like to see is, you know, we need to deliver quality experience and education for all the students in the district.

654
00:49:13,480 --> 00:49:24,480
But I want to, you know, I ran for the seat because I want to see that happen with some stability for the taxpayers.

655
00:49:24,480 --> 00:49:31,480
You know, if you go back and look at 2015 through 2023, the average budget increase was one and a half percent.

656
00:49:31,480 --> 00:49:36,480
So after that, it's, that's been blown out of the water.

657
00:49:36,480 --> 00:49:39,480
We had 16% after that, 4% after that.

658
00:49:39,480 --> 00:49:45,480
We had the anomaly for fiscal year 26, and now we've had another seven and a half percent increase.

659
00:49:45,480 --> 00:49:49,480
For Danville, that means all our taxes are going up double that.

660
00:49:49,480 --> 00:49:53,480
If it's 7%, it's 12 to 14%.

661
00:49:53,480 --> 00:50:03,480
So when we're talking about capital and some of the things that we've caught up on over the last few years, that's fantastic.

662
00:50:03,480 --> 00:50:09,480
It's wonderful to hear that we're maybe there's a light at the end of the tunnel with some of that stuff.

663
00:50:09,480 --> 00:50:19,480
And I think that we should, we need to, you know, do what we can do to, to put our best foot forward for, for, for the future.

664
00:50:19,480 --> 00:50:26,480
But let's not, let's also not be adding in wants that are not needs.

665
00:50:26,480 --> 00:50:43,480
Cause I'd like to get, like I said, I'd like to see these, these year over year increases stabilized to something that is more tolerable for particularly Danville and Sandown, but all four towns.

666
00:50:43,480 --> 00:50:47,480
So that's where I'm at.

667
00:50:47,480 --> 00:50:48,480
You're up.

668
00:50:48,480 --> 00:50:51,480
So I'll just base my comments on kind of three areas.

669
00:50:51,480 --> 00:50:55,480
First thought in terms of administration, I've heard from the public in general.

670
00:50:55,480 --> 00:50:58,480
There's some, there seems to be a sense that we're administration heavy.

671
00:50:58,480 --> 00:51:02,480
I really don't know without having studied it, whether that's true or not.

672
00:51:02,480 --> 00:51:06,480
I think overall administration is a relatively small portion of overall staff.

673
00:51:06,480 --> 00:51:12,480
So as we get to that section, I'd be curious to know, you know, are those criticisms valid?

674
00:51:12,480 --> 00:51:13,480
Is there any truth to that?

675
00:51:13,480 --> 00:51:18,480
I just want to make fact-based decisions on that, you know, based on statewide metrics.

676
00:51:18,480 --> 00:51:22,480
Look at our administration overhead costs relative to the state as a whole.

677
00:51:22,480 --> 00:51:24,480
And are we in line with where we should be?

678
00:51:24,480 --> 00:51:27,480
You know, when they say there's so much that could be cut, is that true?

679
00:51:27,480 --> 00:51:29,480
Is there any validity to that?

680
00:51:29,480 --> 00:51:31,480
I want to look at that.

681
00:51:31,480 --> 00:51:35,480
Number two, in terms of facilities at CIP, this is my first year.

682
00:51:35,480 --> 00:51:38,480
So I'm coming into this fresh with an open mind.

683
00:51:38,480 --> 00:51:44,480
I generally agree with, you know, what Jack said as far as I'm not a big fan of too much deferred maintenance.

684
00:51:44,480 --> 00:51:46,480
That comes back to bite us.

685
00:51:46,480 --> 00:51:52,480
But at the same time, I agree with Sheila's comments regarding, you know, we need to look at each item every single year,

686
00:51:52,480 --> 00:51:58,480
use our best judgment and be as discerning as possible to say, you know, do we really need that or not?

687
00:51:58,480 --> 00:52:03,480
And then finally, in terms of staff and programs, you know, at the end of the day, if we come up with a number,

688
00:52:03,480 --> 00:52:09,480
if that number gets slashed at the limit of what's going to happen is there's going to be cuts to staff and programs.

689
00:52:09,480 --> 00:52:14,480
Coming into this, I don't feel like we're too overstaffed, but we have to go through this, you know,

690
00:52:14,480 --> 00:52:17,480
with an open mind and just look at this and look at the data.

691
00:52:17,480 --> 00:52:23,480
I think that's one of the first agenda items we've got coming up in one of our meetings is to look at overall staffing and headcount.

692
00:52:23,480 --> 00:52:26,480
So I'm willing to look at it with an open mind.

693
00:52:26,480 --> 00:52:31,480
And I think we need to spend a lot of time on that area because that's a significant piece of the budget.

694
00:52:31,480 --> 00:52:35,480
Okay. I'm on budget committee. It's my turn now.

695
00:52:35,480 --> 00:52:42,480
I agree wholeheartedly with you, Marianne, that second statement is perfect.

696
00:52:42,480 --> 00:52:54,480
And I am also thrilled that we're looking at proficiencies because that proficiency rate is terrible in my world.

697
00:52:54,480 --> 00:53:01,480
We need to give the students absolutely what they need. Absolutely. All day one.

698
00:53:01,480 --> 00:53:07,480
However, we got to make sure that people can feed their families and put oil in their tanks

699
00:53:07,480 --> 00:53:13,480
because things have also jumped up economically.

700
00:53:13,480 --> 00:53:19,480
I mean, you're talking 150 gallons. That was last year. It was $400 more this year.

701
00:53:19,480 --> 00:53:34,480
It's money. So it has to be a beautiful balance of what this district needs without leaving $7 million in surplus,

702
00:53:34,480 --> 00:53:40,480
without leaving $16 million in surplus, $15 million in surplus.

703
00:53:40,480 --> 00:53:45,480
And I want to see those again. We need to tighten it up.

704
00:53:45,480 --> 00:53:49,480
$4 million? Three to four, I'd see. I see that.

705
00:53:49,480 --> 00:53:54,480
$16, $7, anything over $4 million shouldn't be happening in my world.

706
00:53:54,480 --> 00:54:01,480
So, yes, that's the job, is to give the kids the best education we possibly can.

707
00:54:01,480 --> 00:54:06,480
But at the same time, we've got to make sure that these kids can eat at home too.

708
00:54:06,480 --> 00:54:11,480
And have lights and have heat and all that good stuff.

709
00:54:11,480 --> 00:54:13,480
That's what we've got.

710
00:54:13,480 --> 00:54:14,480
Now, Jack.

711
00:54:14,480 --> 00:54:15,480
Everyone welcome.

712
00:54:15,480 --> 00:54:16,480
Thank you.

713
00:54:16,480 --> 00:54:18,480
Jack did ask.

714
00:54:18,480 --> 00:54:26,480
So, even though I represent Atkinson, it doesn't get lost upon me some of the challenges of a few of the other communities.

715
00:54:26,480 --> 00:54:31,480
I mean, there's just a reality of the nature of that beast, and we're all in it together.

716
00:54:31,480 --> 00:54:34,480
With that said, I hear all these philosophies today.

717
00:54:34,480 --> 00:54:41,480
The job of the budget committee, I've sat on tons of budget committees and boards and all that, is to adequately fund.

718
00:54:41,480 --> 00:54:43,480
It's not to cut.

719
00:54:43,480 --> 00:54:45,480
It's not to put in a new field house.

720
00:54:45,480 --> 00:54:49,480
It's not to do anything else other than adequately fund.

721
00:54:49,480 --> 00:54:54,480
And those budgets should reflect the values of the communities in which we live, right?

722
00:54:54,480 --> 00:54:58,480
So, if we feel very strongly, well, we have the PAC center.

723
00:54:58,480 --> 00:55:02,480
That was a reflection of the community's wishes.

724
00:55:02,480 --> 00:55:10,480
So, I agree with you, Sheila, that the education of the kids has always got to come first.

725
00:55:10,480 --> 00:55:11,480
And you've got to adequately fund that.

726
00:55:11,480 --> 00:55:20,480
Now, just for the sake of argument in the audience, 70%, Maria, correct me if I'm wrong, but approximately 70% of that budget is non-negotiable.

727
00:55:20,480 --> 00:55:24,480
So, it's contracts, it's wages, salaries.

728
00:55:24,480 --> 00:55:26,480
Is that approximately the right number?

729
00:55:26,480 --> 00:55:28,480
So, that gives you 30%.

730
00:55:28,480 --> 00:55:30,480
That means the gas for the tractors.

731
00:55:30,480 --> 00:55:33,480
That means, you know, the Windex for the wall.

732
00:55:33,480 --> 00:55:37,480
So, it's not even like you have a lot to play with there.

733
00:55:37,480 --> 00:55:41,480
And then if you look at inflation, you know, I heard some numbers being tossed out.

734
00:55:41,480 --> 00:55:45,480
Inflation hits the district just like it hits your households.

735
00:55:45,480 --> 00:55:47,480
Exactly the same.

736
00:55:47,480 --> 00:55:56,480
And I can tell you, looking at some of the projects that we did that we were looking at 10 years ago, you know, we've paid an extra $300,000 for that project.

737
00:55:56,480 --> 00:56:00,480
There were some things like the athletic wing, a complete disgrace.

738
00:56:00,480 --> 00:56:07,480
I had a gentleman who's a COO of a major New Hampshire company, the referee sports.

739
00:56:07,480 --> 00:56:08,480
They put him in a closet.

740
00:56:08,480 --> 00:56:10,480
The closet had no heat to referee.

741
00:56:10,480 --> 00:56:14,480
Then he went to take a shower after and the showers didn't work and they were disgusting.

742
00:56:14,480 --> 00:56:17,480
He says, I'll never come back to Timberland ever again in my life.

743
00:56:17,480 --> 00:56:24,480
Now, for us to do anything because we didn't do it when we had a chance 15 years ago, I couldn't justify doing that right now.

744
00:56:24,480 --> 00:56:25,480
It would be cost prohibitive.

745
00:56:25,480 --> 00:56:28,480
It would be too much of an impact to the taxpayers.

746
00:56:28,480 --> 00:56:33,480
And I don't think anybody is asking for anything like that.

747
00:56:33,480 --> 00:56:45,480
The only thing I can tell you, in my opinion, everything that you'll be presented with this year, this, during that CIP thing, will be a need.

748
00:56:45,480 --> 00:56:50,480
Now, you may prioritize those needs and say, all right, we can live without this for another year or two years.

749
00:56:50,480 --> 00:56:52,480
But some of those things have been on there for 10 years.

750
00:56:52,480 --> 00:56:57,480
It's an ongoing struggle for the staff and the students.

751
00:56:57,480 --> 00:57:00,480
So at some point in time, those things have got to come up.

752
00:57:00,480 --> 00:57:03,480
Fire suppression, not necessarily a want.

753
00:57:03,480 --> 00:57:04,480
It's a need.

754
00:57:04,480 --> 00:57:07,480
Our buildings are falling apart.

755
00:57:07,480 --> 00:57:11,480
The outside the envelope really deteriorating at a rapid rate.

756
00:57:11,480 --> 00:57:20,480
And if you let that go, then we will be spending an extra $2 million to do the same work in five years that we plan on doing this year.

757
00:57:20,480 --> 00:57:28,480
So, again, the job here is to adequately fund the district and education.

758
00:57:28,480 --> 00:57:29,480
It isn't cut.

759
00:57:29,480 --> 00:57:30,480
I hear that a lot on the CIP.

760
00:57:30,480 --> 00:57:33,480
Well, we're going to do this instead.

761
00:57:33,480 --> 00:57:34,480
Adequately fund.

762
00:57:34,480 --> 00:57:36,480
That's the name of the game.

763
00:57:36,480 --> 00:57:40,480
So, and we all will have differences of opinions on what is adequate.

764
00:57:40,480 --> 00:57:42,480
It's kind of subjective to a point.

765
00:57:42,480 --> 00:57:44,480
And that's where the administration comes in.

766
00:57:44,480 --> 00:57:45,480
So that's it.

767
00:57:45,480 --> 00:57:46,480
Thank you.

768
00:57:46,480 --> 00:57:47,480
Sheila.

769
00:57:47,480 --> 00:57:48,480
Go ahead.

770
00:57:48,480 --> 00:57:55,480
I just wanted to put a little sunshine into this.

771
00:57:55,480 --> 00:58:08,480
If about, I never would have predicted we'd be probably 30% to 40% smaller as a total number of students coming from the district as we were in 2006.

772
00:58:08,480 --> 00:58:12,480
But it's been a steady decline.

773
00:58:12,480 --> 00:58:15,480
At the high school, for example, we're under 900.

774
00:58:15,480 --> 00:58:21,480
And in 2006, we were just shy of 1,600.

775
00:58:21,480 --> 00:58:29,480
So it's a significant change in the amount of space needs.

776
00:58:29,480 --> 00:58:35,480
We were having one-way hallways in 2006 to get kids place to place.

777
00:58:35,480 --> 00:58:42,480
We had to expand the passing time because the hallways were too narrow for that number of people.

778
00:58:42,480 --> 00:58:47,480
And the bathrooms were, we were in crisis.

779
00:58:47,480 --> 00:58:49,480
And we were leaking from the roof.

780
00:58:49,480 --> 00:58:54,480
And we were, the gym, we had to close sometimes because there's too much water that came down the walls and onto the floor.

781
00:58:54,480 --> 00:58:55,480
We were a mess.

782
00:58:55,480 --> 00:59:00,480
We had to change all the flooring in the whole building because it was asbestos written.

783
00:59:00,480 --> 00:59:03,480
We had all types of issues.

784
00:59:03,480 --> 00:59:12,480
Most of those issues have been addressed and a good portion of them have been addressed just through a decrease in the number of kids in the building.

785
00:59:12,480 --> 00:59:15,480
So this could be much worse.

786
00:59:15,480 --> 00:59:21,480
Do you think there's a correlation between the decrease in the number of students in the building because of the poor facilities?

787
00:59:21,480 --> 00:59:28,480
Like if I, when I was looking to move somewhere, I'm looking at the schools, not only at the performance, but where my kids are going to play sports and things like that.

788
00:59:28,480 --> 00:59:32,480
Are families, is this impacting our values if we're not investing in the schools?

789
00:59:32,480 --> 00:59:37,480
Is my home value dropping because people are choosing not to live here?

790
00:59:37,480 --> 00:59:42,480
I can't with the degree of insurance.

791
00:59:42,480 --> 00:59:43,480
Yeah.

792
00:59:43,480 --> 00:59:57,480
My sense is, is that there are families that are looking for specific things that, you know, they might find another school in better shape or it meets the kids they have there or their priorities.

793
00:59:57,480 --> 01:00:03,480
But I don't have any data on that, even having kind of seen that exodus.

794
01:00:03,480 --> 01:00:06,480
I, I, I couldn't account for all of it.

795
01:00:06,480 --> 01:00:16,480
Um, some of it's just the economy and what happened in, uh, 2010 and 12, some economic pieces that really hit us and changed the district.

796
01:00:16,480 --> 01:00:18,480
And it's just attrition.

797
01:00:18,480 --> 01:00:23,480
Um, we don't have to buy people are buying a lot of homes in some of our towns.

798
01:00:23,480 --> 01:00:30,480
So I'm expecting that we might see some regeneration through elementary right now and later on at the high school.

799
01:00:30,480 --> 01:00:33,480
But, uh, right now we've been projected.

800
01:00:33,480 --> 01:00:38,480
I think when we look at NASDAQ reports to be fairly stable for the next few years.

801
01:00:38,480 --> 01:00:55,480
So, um, you know, that's, uh, from the perspective of holding a budget down, at least it's not like we're going the other direction where we're increasing at a pace that we have to have more space, more, more facilities, more staff.

802
01:00:55,480 --> 01:01:01,480
But we haven't decreased staff significantly over the last couple of budgets.

803
01:01:01,480 --> 01:01:17,480
Um, and, uh, so I, I think we're kind of, we were talking about things that in the, in the board about what we're not providing or what we feel we're not doing a good enough job with those.

804
01:01:17,480 --> 01:01:22,480
There are some realities there as demands change into special education.

805
01:01:22,480 --> 01:01:28,480
Somebody mentioned, uh, you know, areas that, uh, you know, we have a mandate to, to do the work.

806
01:01:28,480 --> 01:01:38,480
It's going to cost us one way or another is either through hiring people or hiring people outside to do the work because by law we have to provide a certain degree of service to meet every kid's need.

807
01:01:38,480 --> 01:01:41,480
So we, we have issues, we have challenges.

808
01:01:41,480 --> 01:01:43,480
I'm not trying to belittle the challenges.

809
01:01:43,480 --> 01:01:52,480
And, and I, I think there is a figure, um, in some people's mind about, you know, how, how our district is perceived.

810
01:01:52,480 --> 01:02:08,480
I, I know, you know, what Jack was talking about because, you know, our facilities in terms of the athletic, uh, you know, walk rooms and that area of the building, um, are as bad as anywhere in the state.

811
01:02:08,480 --> 01:02:12,480
You know, and I've been in most of the buildings in this state.

812
01:02:12,480 --> 01:02:24,480
Um, and not that everybody has the Taj Mahal, but yeah, most people have, uh, kind of parity between what's available for men and women, boys and girls.

813
01:02:24,480 --> 01:02:34,480
Um, you know, they have certain ratios that they try to, you know, stay within in terms of, you know, places and spaces, you know, to, to meet the needs of all people.

814
01:02:34,480 --> 01:02:39,480
We, we fall short in a lot of those facility ways.

815
01:02:39,480 --> 01:02:42,480
And so it's going to take some time to change that.

816
01:02:42,480 --> 01:02:48,480
So, but you've seen a lot with the, you know, the work that's gone on in the last few years, which is pretty commendable.

817
01:02:48,480 --> 01:02:49,480
We've gotten a lot done.

818
01:02:49,480 --> 01:02:52,480
We're getting that capital improvement list.

819
01:02:52,480 --> 01:02:53,480
Yeah.

820
01:02:53,480 --> 01:02:56,480
It's about the third of the size it was three years ago.

821
01:02:56,480 --> 01:02:57,480
Now that's pretty cool.

822
01:02:57,480 --> 01:03:03,480
Um, you know, so, I mean, there are, there is good work going on here to try to meet this, you know, period of time, which is very difficult for me.

823
01:03:03,480 --> 01:03:07,480
This, this district didn't create.

824
01:03:07,480 --> 01:03:08,480
Right.

825
01:03:08,480 --> 01:03:16,480
We don't create, create the, the, the oil prices that you talked about, you know, we, we, but it's a reality.

826
01:03:16,480 --> 01:03:17,480
It is a reality.

827
01:03:17,480 --> 01:03:18,480
Right.

828
01:03:18,480 --> 01:03:29,480
So here we are, you know, but I think we're on the same side here, you know, the board and the budget committee in terms of what Marianne, you know, said in terms of trying to find the balance.

829
01:03:29,480 --> 01:03:38,480
You know, to, to say, well, how can we continue to, you know, provide what we feel is a good education for all our kids and meet all our kids needs.

830
01:03:38,480 --> 01:03:44,480
Um, and then, you know, you know, what is, what is the priority priority each year?

831
01:03:44,480 --> 01:03:49,480
Uh, so like Sheila talked about, you know, I want to make sure we take care of the most important things first.

832
01:03:49,480 --> 01:03:51,480
I think we're all on the same page about that.

833
01:03:51,480 --> 01:03:52,480
No?

834
01:03:52,480 --> 01:03:53,480
Yes.

835
01:03:53,480 --> 01:03:54,480
Yeah.

836
01:03:54,480 --> 01:03:55,480
Yeah.

837
01:03:55,480 --> 01:04:02,480
Actually going off some of what you had said, I'd be interested in, um, the school board looking more into like, especially at significant cost.

838
01:04:02,480 --> 01:04:03,480
Right.

839
01:04:03,480 --> 01:04:16,480
Obviously we have to fund those, but I wonder if there are things that can be done to offset it earlier intervention things, things building into, like, other ways to support full date, like full date kindergarten and other early things like that.

840
01:04:16,480 --> 01:04:17,480
Yes.

841
01:04:17,480 --> 01:04:19,480
Um, but even prior to that, you know what I mean?

842
01:04:19,480 --> 01:04:22,480
Like our responsibility begins at age three, right?

843
01:04:22,480 --> 01:04:23,480
Oh, I agree with you.

844
01:04:23,480 --> 01:04:28,480
Some people don't realize though that it's age three, that the school district becomes responsible for special education services.

845
01:04:28,480 --> 01:04:35,480
So if we're investing in spending some money then to spend less money over time, it helps the district.

846
01:04:35,480 --> 01:04:43,480
So are there things that the district are doing or looking into that we could invest in, in order to offset the cost, the longterm?

847
01:04:43,480 --> 01:04:44,480
Cause it's significant.

848
01:04:44,480 --> 01:04:52,480
Those costs include, you know, out of district placements, special busing, Paris specialist, you know, obviously contracted services.

849
01:04:52,480 --> 01:04:56,480
Are there things that we could be doing sooner to help with that?

850
01:04:56,480 --> 01:05:04,480
Oh, and some of the things I've seen going on because we, we take a look at who's hired and what their backgrounds are.

851
01:05:04,480 --> 01:05:10,480
Is it done a much better job in the last couple of years in terms of who we're hiring?

852
01:05:10,480 --> 01:05:17,480
We've been very fortunate in able to find highly qualified people for positions that we've been able to afford to fill.

853
01:05:17,480 --> 01:05:27,480
You know, I think the scrutiny of the quality of what we bring into the district is the most important aspect of ensuring ongoing success.

854
01:05:27,480 --> 01:05:34,480
You need the most important single impact you can have on a child's learning is the teacher.

855
01:05:34,480 --> 01:05:44,480
You have, you can't not hire somebody who's good and hire somebody who's less good, strictly on money.

856
01:05:44,480 --> 01:05:50,480
You have to look at what they do to help, you know, the school work, you know, things like that.

857
01:05:50,480 --> 01:05:59,480
So I, I do think the things that we can control are looking out for the quality of the work that we choose to do or have to do.

858
01:05:59,480 --> 01:06:07,480
And I, I feel like the voters, the last time people kept coming up and specifically mentioning, they didn't like certain positions that were cut, including Paris.

859
01:06:07,480 --> 01:06:12,480
Um, library pieces, you know, there were some comments I remember.

860
01:06:12,480 --> 01:06:26,480
Um, but, um, if there was positions are directed to help with the special ed or the offsetting, even for typical, cause it shouldn't, you know, those kids also need to be reaching their fullest potential sooner.

861
01:06:26,480 --> 01:06:30,480
And then maybe those costs later on, you know what I mean?

862
01:06:30,480 --> 01:06:33,480
Uh, just want to make sure that we're still in a good position there for that.

863
01:06:33,480 --> 01:06:52,480
Oh, absolutely. All the, all the work that goes into understanding what is essential in a person's IEP that, or, and how often we return IPs to see how kids have, once they've learned, uh, to deal with a certain, a challenge that they're able to, uh, do that perhaps without the same degree of support moving forward.

864
01:06:52,480 --> 01:07:04,480
There are ways to try to, you know, be current in your assessments of what people need so that you're not wasting service on somebody who's already outgrown that need.

865
01:07:04,480 --> 01:07:16,480
So there are things like that, that I, I see people doing, which is really outstanding, um, working in some, it's a, it's a challenge, but it does save money over time.

866
01:07:16,480 --> 01:07:17,480
Okay.

867
01:07:17,480 --> 01:07:18,480
All right.

868
01:07:18,480 --> 01:07:19,480
I'm good.

869
01:07:19,480 --> 01:07:20,480
Okay.

870
01:07:20,480 --> 01:07:26,480
I bet that everybody's had an opportunity to speak.

871
01:07:26,480 --> 01:07:27,480
Correct?

872
01:07:27,480 --> 01:07:28,480
Shawna?

873
01:07:28,480 --> 01:07:29,480
Nothing?

874
01:07:29,480 --> 01:07:32,480
It would just be echoing a lot of sentiments.

875
01:07:32,480 --> 01:07:33,480
Okay.

876
01:07:33,480 --> 01:07:34,480
Let's do the same thing.

877
01:07:34,480 --> 01:07:35,480
Okay.

878
01:07:35,480 --> 01:07:37,480
Are we good?

879
01:07:37,480 --> 01:07:39,480
Anybody else have any comments?

880
01:07:39,480 --> 01:07:40,480
Short and sweet, so we can get in.

881
01:07:40,480 --> 01:07:41,480
Short and sweet.

882
01:07:41,480 --> 01:07:42,480
I'd be interested.

883
01:07:42,480 --> 01:07:45,480
In the past, there's been offerings of a facilities review.

884
01:07:45,480 --> 01:07:50,480
Our tour paired with, uh, you know, review of current CIP priorities.

885
01:07:50,480 --> 01:07:51,480
What's been done in the past?

886
01:07:51,480 --> 01:07:52,480
What's planned for the near future?

887
